---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Services — IonPath" current="/guides/services/">
  <div class="animate-fade-in-up">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-2">Services</h1>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-8">Services define RPC interfaces with typed methods. They generate client proxies, server executors, and CBOR formatters for each method's arguments and return type.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-12 animate-fade-in-up-delay-1">

    <section>
      <h2 id="basic-syntax" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Basic Syntax</h2>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-2">/// Integer arithmetic over a fixed left-hand operand.</span>
<span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">MathInteraction</span>(leftOperand: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>) {'{'}
    <span class="text-accent-2">/// Adds rightOperand to the bound operand.</span>
    Add(rightOperand: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>): <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>;
    <span class="text-accent-2">/// Multiplies the bound operand by rightOperand.</span>
    Mul(rightOperand: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>): <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">A service has:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-sm text-text-secondary mt-2">
        <li><strong>Name</strong> — identifier for the service interface</li>
        <li><strong>Base arguments</strong> — shared parameters for all methods (in parentheses after the name)</li>
        <li><strong>Methods</strong> — named RPC operations with typed parameters and return type</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="base-arguments" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Base Arguments</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Base arguments are declared in the service header and are automatically prepended to every method call. They act like a constructor or context that applies to all methods:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">UserService</span>(
    <span class="text-accent-2">/// Tenant the call is scoped to.</span>
    spaceId: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>,
    <span class="text-accent-2">/// Bearer token for the calling principal.</span>
    auth: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>
) {'{'}
    GetUser(userId: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>): <span class="type-name">User</span>;
    <span class="text-text-muted">// On the wire, GetUser actually sends: [spaceId, auth, userId]</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">In C#, base arguments become constructor parameters. In TypeScript, they are passed when creating the service client. Arguments accept <a href="/guides/comments/" class="text-accent hover:underline">doc comments</a> of their own, which become <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">&lt;param&gt;</code> / <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">{'@'}param</code> entries in the generated code.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="method-types" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Method Types</h2>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Unary (default)</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3">Standard request-response. One request, one response over HTTP POST:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">DataService</span>() {'{'}
    Do(data: <span class="type-keyword">bytes</span>);           <span class="text-text-muted">// void return</span>
    DoIt(data: <span class="type-keyword">bytes</span>): <span class="type-keyword">bytes</span>;  <span class="text-text-muted">// returns bytes</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Route: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">POST /ion/DataService/DoIt.unary</code></p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Server Streaming</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3">The <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">stream</code> modifier on the return type creates a server-streaming method over WebSocket:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">EventService</span>(seed: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>) {'{'}
    <span class="text-accent-3">stream</span> Integer(i: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>): <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">In C#, the return type becomes <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">IAsyncEnumerable&lt;i4&gt;</code>. Route: <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">/ion/EventService/Integer.ws</code></p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Bidirectional Streaming</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3">Add the <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">stream</code> modifier to a parameter for bidirectional streaming:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">StreamService</span>(seed: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>) {'{'}
    <span class="text-accent-3">stream</span> Floats(<span class="text-accent-3">stream</span> i: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>): <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Both the input parameter and the return are streams. Only <strong>one</strong> parameter per method can be marked as <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">stream</code> (violation: <a href="/guides/diagnostics/" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0013</a>).</p>

      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">The default is unary, and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">unary</code> may be written explicitly for symmetry with <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">stream</code>. It is the spelling of the default and changes nothing:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">DataService</span>() {'{'}
    <span class="text-accent-3">unary</span> DoIt(data: <span class="type-keyword">bytes</span>): <span class="type-keyword">bytes</span>;  <span class="text-text-muted">// identical to omitting 'unary'</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mt-8 mb-3">Internal Methods</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3">A method marked <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">internal</code> is <strong>not part of the generated client's public API</strong>. It is still declared on the service interface, still dispatched by the server, and still carried on the wire unchanged — only the generated client stops advertising it. The effect is that a peer service compiled alongside the contracts can still call it, while an outside consumer of the client package cannot.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-3"><strong>It is not a security boundary.</strong> The route is unchanged and the server still accepts it, so anything that can reach the endpoint can call the method. It is a visibility control over generated code, nothing more.</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">AdminService</span>() {'{'}
    GetStatus(): <span class="type-name">Status</span>;
    <span class="text-accent-3">internal</span> Migrate();     <span class="text-text-muted">// server-side only</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4 mb-2">The method is never <em>deleted</em> from the output — it is made invisible using each target's own native visibility mechanism, so the generated code still compiles and the server side is untouched:</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-[12px] border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Target</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Mechanism</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 font-medium">Result</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-text-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
              <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium text-text-primary">C#</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-3">Explicit interface implementation — <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">async Task I{'{'}Service{'}'}.Migrate()</code> instead of <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">public async Task Migrate()</code></td>
              <td class="py-2">Off the concrete client type's surface. Still reachable by casting the client to <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">I{'{'}Service{'}'}</code>, which is how a peer service calls it.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-white/5">
              <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium text-text-primary">TypeScript</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-3">A JSDoc <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">{'@'}internal</code> tag on the method</td>
              <td class="py-2">TypeScript cannot narrow the visibility of a member an <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">implements</code> clause requires, so the method stays on the class and <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">tsc --stripInternal</code> removes it from the published <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">.d.ts</code> — the actual client surface.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium text-text-primary">Rust</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-3">Drops <code class="font-mono text-[11px]">pub</code></td>
              <td class="py-2">Crate private. The client struct implements no trait, so nothing obliges the method to stay public; a peer in the same crate can call it, the crate's public API cannot.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">Three things deliberately do <em>not</em> change. The <strong>service interface</strong> still declares the method, so a server implementation must still provide it. The <strong>server executor / router</strong> still dispatches it. And the <strong><a href="/guides/schema-lock/" class="text-accent hover:underline">schema lock</a></strong> records it like any other method, so removing an internal method is still <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0025" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0025</a>. To the compiler, an <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">internal</code> method is routed and validated exactly like any other — the distinction exists only in code generation.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">The modifier previously did nothing at all: it parsed, was carried through the compiler and written into <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">ion.lock.json</code>, and no generator ever read it — so a method marked <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">internal</code> appeared in the generated client exactly like any other. This page used to claim it was "not exposed in public interfaces", which was never true of any target.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="void-methods" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Void Methods</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Methods that don't return a value omit the return type or use <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">void</code>:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">NotificationService</span>() {'{'}
    SendNotification(userId: <span class="type-keyword">u4</span>, text: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>);  <span class="text-text-muted">// void — no return type</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="generated-code" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Generated Code</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A service generates multiple artifacts:</p>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-grid-header">
          <span>Artifact</span>
          <span>Description</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-name">IServiceName</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">C# interface extending <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">IIonService</code></span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-name">Ion_ServiceName_ClientImpl</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Client proxy — serializes args, calls remote, deserializes result</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px] type-name">Ion_ServiceName_ServiceExecutor</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Server-side router — deserializes request, invokes impl, serializes response</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]">formatters</span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">CBOR read/write methods for each method's argument tuple</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="real-world" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Real-World Example</h2>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Vector</span> {'{'}
    x: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>;
    y: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>;
    z: <span class="type-keyword">f4</span>;
{'}'}

<span class="text-accent-3">service</span> <span class="type-name">VectorMathInteraction</span>(leftOperand: <span class="type-name">Vector</span>) {'{'}
    Abs(): <span class="type-name">Vector</span>;
    Add(rightOperand: <span class="type-name">Vector</span>): <span class="type-name">Vector</span>;
    Clamp(min: <span class="type-name">Vector</span>, max: <span class="type-name">Vector</span>): <span class="type-name">Vector</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</DocsLayout>
